\chapter{Pixels, Networks, and the X Window System}
\label{ch:x}
\index{X Window System}\index{bitmap terminals}\index{window managers}\index{Blit}

\section{A bitmap changes what a screen can mean}

A character terminal begins with a grid whose cells have predefined roles.
A bitmap display begins with addressable picture elements.
Text becomes one kind of image among many. Windows can overlap,
proportional fonts can appear beside diagrams, and a pointer can select
objects that are not conveniently described by a line number.

Experiments at Bell Labs, including the Blit terminal associated with Rob
Pike and colleagues, explored programmable bitmap terminals and layers of
interaction. Computation could be divided between host and terminal in
ways that did not fit the old picture of a passive display.
A terminal could become a small computing environment in its own right.

These experiments should not be turned into a single chain in which curses
necessarily evolved into Blit and then into X. Different groups pursued
related problems with different hardware and assumptions.
The relationship is a change in the available design space, not always
a direct code lineage \parencite{kernighan-history,raymond}.

\section{Why the display is the server}

X emerged at MIT in the 1980s, with Bob Scheifler and Jim Gettys among its
central figures. It offered a network-capable protocol between graphical
applications and a display service. The application is the X client;
the component managing the display and input is the X server.
This terminology follows the service being provided, not the physical
size or location of the machine.

An application running on a distant host can connect to a server on the
user's workstation. The user sees a local window while the application
executes elsewhere. This fits the workstation era's mixture of personal
interaction and shared computation. It also explains why the server is
not necessarily the large remote machine in everyday language.

X11, introduced in 1987, established a protocol whose longevity became
remarkable. Implementations and extensions changed, but the protocol
boundary allowed a huge body of clients and tools to accumulate.
A stable protocol can preserve investments across hardware generations
in much the same way that a stable file interface can preserve applications.

Network transparency was valuable, but it did not make distance free.
Round trips add latency. A program that repeatedly asks the server for
information and waits before issuing the next request can perform poorly
over a long link. A protocol permitting remote execution does not guarantee
that every application uses it efficiently.

\section{The server does not choose the entire desktop}

X provides mechanisms for windows, events, drawing, and related resources.
A window manager can decide placement, decoration, stacking, and focus
policy. Toolkits provide widgets and application conventions.
Desktop environments assemble still more services, including file
management, settings, session behavior, and integration among applications.

This division creates flexibility. A user can change the window manager
without replacing every client. A toolkit can offer a new visual style
without requiring the server to implement every button's behavior.
Different institutions can choose interaction policies suited to their
users and hardware.

It also creates integration costs. Selection and clipboard conventions,
keyboard focus, drag and drop, fonts, settings, and session management
need agreements across components that were designed independently.
A protocol that permits many policies does not supply a shared policy
by itself. Users can experience that absence as inconsistency
\parencite[discussion of X and mechanism versus policy]{raymond}.

Desktop projects such as KDE and GNOME addressed some of these needs by
building more coherent environments on top of the display system.
Their importance extends beyond visual decoration. Common libraries,
accessibility interfaces, settings mechanisms, and application conventions
make the platform easier to use and develop for.

\section{Rendering moves away from the original model}
\index{OpenGL}\index{Mesa}\index{direct rendering}

Early X offered drawing requests suited to the hardware and applications
of its time. Later graphics workloads demanded accelerated 3D rendering,
complex compositing, modern font rasterization, and efficient image
transfer. Asking the server to perform every rendering operation through
the original protocol was no longer the best path.

Direct-rendering infrastructure let applications use graphics hardware
through controlled interfaces while the display system retained
coordination responsibilities. OpenGL and later APIs supplied rendering
models very different from drawing a rectangle through core X requests.
Mesa became a major collection of open graphics implementations and
userspace drivers on Linux and other systems.

The resulting stack contains several distinct roles. A kernel driver
manages hardware access and resources. A userspace driver translates
rendering operations into device commands. Libraries expose APIs to
applications. A display server and compositor coordinate visible surfaces.
The same vendor name can appear in several layers, but those layers are
not interchangeable.

This is why a report that ``X renders everything'' can be misleading for
a modern desktop. Many applications already render their own content
using local libraries and acceleration, then arrange for the result to
be displayed. The protocol's historical identity and the actual route
of a frame can differ substantially.

\section{Compositing adds another view of the screen}
\index{compositors}

A compositing window manager can combine off-screen window contents into
a final image. This enables effects such as shadows, translucency,
transforms, and smoother transitions. It also provides a place to manage
presentation timing and the relationship between application updates and
the display's refresh.

The extra stage can be useful, but it raises questions about copying,
latency, and synchronization. A buffer must not be displayed before
rendering finishes, and an application must not overwrite storage still
being used for presentation. Graphics correctness includes ownership
and timing agreements as much as pixel calculations.

Full-screen applications can sometimes avoid unnecessary composition
through direct-scanout or related paths, subject to hardware and policy.
There is no universal rule that compositing always adds the same amount
of latency or that bypassing it is always possible.
The implementation must consider format, scaling, overlays, multiple
monitors, and other clients.

The growing complexity motivated a reconsideration of where the display
boundary should sit. If clients render their own content and a compositor
already decides what reaches the screen, why retain all the historical
coordination paths between client, X server, window manager, and compositor?
Wayland would make that question central.

\section{Trust in a shared display connection}
\index{X Window System!security}

The traditional X model assumes considerable trust among clients connected
to a server. Depending on extensions and controls, clients can obtain
information or influence input in ways inappropriate for mutually hostile
applications. A desktop running every application under one user's broad
authority inherits that assumption.

Authentication to the server is therefore critical.
Opening access broadly to solve a connection problem can grant far more
power than the user intended. Remote forwarding through SSH protects a
transport and can apply trust distinctions, but it does not turn the whole
application environment into a capability system automatically.

This security model is historically understandable. A workstation user's
programs were often treated as collaborators in one personal environment.
Modern desktops install applications from many sources, process hostile
web content, and offer sandboxing. A display protocol that assumes all
clients are equally trusted can undermine those boundaries.

The criticism is not that X developers failed to predict every later
threat. It is that a once-useful trust model became an architectural
constraint. Long-lived interfaces preserve assumptions along with features.
Replacing those assumptions requires migration work that can be as hard
as designing the new protocol.

\section{What X accomplished}

X gave Unix a broadly shared graphical substrate across vendors and
architectures. It supported local and remote applications, a wide variety
of window managers, and decades of software. That achievement is not
cancelled by the difficulty of adapting it to new rendering and security
models.

The complaints in the supplied \emph{UNIX-HATERS Handbook} remain useful
as evidence of costs users felt while the system was still relatively
young. They should be read beside, rather than in place of, an account of
what the architecture enabled \parencite[ch. 7]{haters}.
A history that records only triumph or only irritation misses the reason
such systems both succeed and become candidates for replacement.

Wayland did not arise because Unix suddenly discovered graphics.
It arose after decades of graphical practice had shifted rendering,
compositing, hardware access, and application trust. Understanding X's
success makes the reasons for that shift more precise.
